he frame; all optical flow lines radiate symmetrically from the optical center of Grid B2. The abandoned forest fire lookout base is now in a fully cleared exterior access state, documented as one continuous photoreal photograph under ambient overcast daylight. The entire visible exterior access zone across Grid C1 through Grid C3 is now fully cleared, with clean damp forest soil, exposed footing edges, and safer walking lanes complete throughout. Persistent traces include rake comb lines, clipped vine ends on lower braces, and two shallow wheelbarrow tire tracks curving away from the stair footings. Locked anchors: rotted stair footings at Grid C2 holding 55 percent of frame height, rusted stair landing railing at Grid B2 holding 48 percent of frame height, peeling lookout cabin doorway at Grid A2 holding 34 percent of frame height.\n\n图片 3:\nstatic elevated-threshold tripod shot, ultra-wide 14mm lens feel, camera height 1.6m, coaxial axis up repaired stairs toward lookout doorway; horizon line remains perfectly level at exactly 50-percent height of the frame; all optical flow lines radiate symmetrically from the optical center of Grid B2. The entire visible lookout exterior stair run and guardrail system is now fully repaired under ambient daylight. The repaired stairs lead safely to the open doorway. Through that threshold, the lookout cabin wraparound window band, repaired roof hatch skylight frame, and original exposed ceiling rafters are already visible in sharp focus, small at about one fifth of frame height. Persistent traces include bright screw heads, sawdust caught on tread edges, and dark rust-converter brush marks on the rail joints. Locked anchors: rotted stair footings at Grid C2 holding 55 percent of frame height, rusted stair landing railing at Grid B2 holding 48 percent of frame height, peeling lookout cabin doorway at Grid A2 holding 34 percent of frame height.\n\n图片 4 [BRIDGE]:\nstatic tripod shot, ultra-wide 14-18mm lens feel, camera height 1.6m