or walls, ceiling, and floor fill the frame edge to edge; camera pitch locked level, central vanishing axis centered. The entire visible old plank floor area is now fully cleared bare and swept, while unrecoated off-white wall boards and original rafters keep their weathered surfaces for later finishing. The roof hatch skylight frame stays repaired overhead and unchanged. Persistent traces include fine sawdust curls along sill bases, glossy amber epoxy darkening at splice edges, and narrow putty ridges around the newly reset panes. Locked anchors: lookout cabin wraparound window band at Grid A1-A3 holding 36 percent of frame height, repaired roof hatch skylight frame at Grid A2 holding 26 percent of frame height, original exposed ceiling rafters at Grid A2 holding 30 percent of frame height.\n\n图片 7:\nInterior walls, ceiling, and floor fill the frame edge to edge, camera pitch locked level and central vanishing axis centered. A small rechargeable battery lantern on a low shelf gives partial practical warmth alongside daylight from the wraparound window band and repaired roof hatch skylight. Across the entire visible floor area, the finished planks now fully cover the surface with a low-sheen matte finish and only heavily blurred, muted, diffused reflections of window light and ceiling lines, never sharp mirror detail. Persistent traces include tight repeated plank seams, small perimeter expansion gaps under the walls, and faint cork dust settled near the room edges. Locked anchors: lookout cabin wraparound window band at Grid A1-A3 holding 36 percent of frame height, repaired roof hatch skylight frame at Grid A2 holding 26 percent of frame height, original exposed ceiling rafters at Grid A2 holding 30 percent of frame height.\n\n图片 8:\nInterior walls, ceiling, and floor fill the frame edge to edge, camera pitch locked level and central vanishing axis centered. The lookout is now in a completed practical-lighting installation state, with the entire visible interior fully fitted by this final practical-lighting operation. Repaired window panes, putty beads, cedar sill splices, roof hatch skylight frame, and exposed rafters all remain in their fixed locations. Final practical lighting has stabilized into an even warm glow, while daylight from the wraparound window band and skylight still names the carrier’s own openings. Persistent traces include tiny screw heads in the strip channels, cable saddle rows, and slight lantern clamp impressions on the posts. Locked anchors: lookout cabin wraparound window band at Grid A1-A3 holding 36 percent of frame height, repaired roof hatch skylight frame at Grid A2 holding 26 percent of frame height, original exposed ceiling rafters at Grid A2 holding 30 percent of frame height.\n\n图片 9:\nInterior walls, ceiling, and floor fill the frame edge to edge, camera pitch locked level and central vanishing axis centered. The restored lookout is now fully furnished across the entire visible interior as a compact stargazing cabin. Ceiling-edge strip channels, puck lights, and clipped wall lanterns glow in dimmed warm stargazing mode while the window band and skylight deepen to night tones. The floor’s low-sheen surface shows heavily blurred, muted reflections of chair legs, warm lights, and window shapes, with no sharp mirror detail. Persistent traces include rug compression lines, tiny chair-foot pressure marks, and round tripod foot pads on the matte finished floor. Locked anchors: lookout cabin wraparound window band at Grid A1-A3 holding 36 percent of frame height, repaired roof hatch skylight frame at Grid A2 holding 26 percent of frame height, original exposed ceiling rafters at Grid A2 holding 30 percent of frame height.\n\n图片 10:\nInterior walls, ceiling, and floor fill the frame edge to edge, camera pitch locked level and central vanishing axis centered. Cedar sill splices, glazing putty beads, clear panes, exposed rafters, and the repaired skylight frame remain fixed and legible. The floor show