c bins, bag plastic crinkling, and wheelbarrow tire crunching over needles; ambient forest wind and distant treetop movement continue steadily left to right.\n\n视频 2:\nUse the provided first frame and last frame as exact composition anchors. Use IMAGE 2 as the actual first-frame image and IMAGE 3 as the actual last-frame image; every visible action must interpolate between those two frame images without inventing a third layout. The static exterior camera keeps the same tower legs, cross-bracing, stair direction, landing railing, cabin doorway, roof hatch profile, and frame boundaries locked. This is continuous construction time-lapse, not real-time footage, holding ambient overcast daylight. At t=0s, one lone worker in a solid bright-neon-yellow safety vest, white hardhat, solid dark blue work pants, gloves, and respirator enters from Grid C1 and climbs the cleaned approach with a pry bar and an impact driver clipped at the belt. The worker repeats tightening motions along both guardrails, wire-brushing rust with a hand wire brush, applying dark rust converter from a small sealed metal cup, and driving galvanized bolts and brackets at loose railing joints. Progress reads clearly as missing dark treads become continuous pale boards from Grid C2 to the landing, and crooked rusted rail segments straighten into parallel climb lines toward the doorway. By t=7.5s, the worker carries the pry bar and last empty hardware bucket out through Grid C1. Persistent traces: countersunk screw heads, pale saw-cut board ends, blackened rust-converter streaks, and fresh galvanized bolt washers. Near-field audio includes pry-bar creaks, impact-driver rattles, wire-brush scraping, and metal bolt clinks; steady forest air and distant branch movement continue underneath.\n\n视频 3 [BRIDGE]:\nUse the provided first frame and last frame as exact composition anchors. Use IMAGE 3 as the actual first-frame image and IMAGE 4 as the actual last-frame image; every visible action must interpolate between those two frame images without inventing a third layout. This is the only visible exterior-to-interior crossing clip, with no separate doorway hold and no resting threshold composition. The same ultra-wide lens feel is maintained while the camera rises smoothly along the repaired stair line and pushes forward at the same steady rate toward the elevated lookout door; no pan, no tilt, no roll during the climb, only a combined forward-and-upward vector. At t=0s, the open doorway is centered ahead, the repaired stairs project upward from the lower frame, and the same interior lookout cabin wraparound window band, repaired roof hatch skylight frame, and original exposed ceiling rafters are already sharp through the opening. The inherited interior anchors scale up continuously along the camera axis, the window band growing from small doorway detail to a broad upper wraparound identity feature, the skylight frame settling overhead, and the rafters holding their spacing without re-rendering. By the final frame, the camera is fully inside; the door frame, door leaf, threshold edges, and entry opening are completely behind camera. Persistent traces carried across the move are clean pressure-treated stair boards behind the approach, glazing edges seen ahead, and dust on old interior planks. Sounds shift from bootstep thumps on exterior treads and faint railing creaks to hollow plank-floor resonance; ambient forest wind softens into enclosed room tone with fine dust drifting left to right.\n\n视频 4:\nUse the provided first frame and last frame as exact composition anchors. Use IMAGE 4 as the actual first-frame image and IMAGE 5 as the actual last-frame image; every visible action must interpolate between those two frame images without inventing a third layout. The camera stays fixed inside the square lookout cabin, level pitch locked, central vanishing axis centered, with the wraparound window band, roof hatch skylight frame, and exposed rafters holding their registered positions. This is continuous construction time-lapse, not real-time footage, with ambient daylight from the cabin’s own openings. At t=0s, one lone worker in a solid bright-neon-yellow safety vest, white hardhat, solid dark blue work pants, gloves, and respirator enters from the camera-side lower edge and stays in low-detail silhouette. The shop vacuum appears only during the active clip, its hose being dragged across plank seams as the worker vacuuming each seam from the window ledges inward; the canister remains a moving transient object, never part of the last frame. Progress is legible as bare old planks appear wall to wall, corner piles shrink into closed containers, and dusty window ledges clear across the full wraparound band. By t=7.5s, the worker carries the broom and final sealed bag out through the camera-side lower edge, followed by the vacuum hose disappearing from frame. Persistent traces: broom streaks in remaining dust, exposed plank seams, small scuffed rectangles where debris bins sat. Near-field sound features bristle scraping, glass clicking inside a lidded bin, vacuum hose bumps, and plastic bag rustle; ambient room tone holds with faint daylight shimmer and dust drifting left to right.\n\n视频 5:\nUse the provided first frame and last frame as exact composition anchors. Use IMAGE 5 as the actual first-frame image and IMAGE 6 as the actual last-frame image; every visible action must interpolate between those two frame images without inventing a third layout. The interior tripod composition remains locked, with the wraparound window band, roof hatch skylight frame, rafters, floor edges, and wall geometry unchanged. This is continuous construction time-lapse, not real-time footage, beginning in ambient daylight and ending with a temporary warm work light glowing softly on the repaired wood and glass. At t=0s, one lone worker in a solid bright-neon-yellow safety vest, white hardhat, solid dark blue work pants, gloves, and respirator enters from the camera-side lower edge carrying an oscillating saw as the single featured cutting tool. Progress reads as dark punky sill scars becoming pale cedar splices across every visible window opening, and cracked glass reflections becoming continuous clean panes under the same fixed frames. A portable battery work light is switched on near the end, warming the repaired sill lines without adding new windows. By t=7.5s, the worker carries the saw, bins, and light stand controls out through the camera-side lower edge, leaving only the steady light glow. Persistent traces: cedar end-grain patches, putty beads, glazing point flecks, and fine sawdust curls. Audio includes oscillating-saw buzzing, pane clinks inside a bin, putty knife taps, and brush swishes; outside wind is muted by the new glass while the interior remains softly resonant.\n\n视频 6:\nUse the provided first frame and last frame as exact composition anchors. Use IMAGE 6 as the actual first-frame image and IMAGE 7 as the actual last-frame image; every visible action must interpolate between those two frame images without inventing a third layout. The interior camera holds its tripod view, level pitch, centered vanishing axis, and fixed upper anchors while the operation is confined to the full visible floor plane. This is continuous construction time-lapse, not real-time footage, starting with a warm temporary work light and ending with a modest battery lantern giving partial practical warmth over the finished floor. At t=0s, one lone worker in a solid bright-neon-yellow safety vest, white hardhat, solid dark blue work pants, gloves, and respirator enters from the camera-side lower edge with a rubber tapping block as the single featured hand tool. The old uneven floor vanishes beneath a level warm matte plank surface from wall to wall, while the fixed windows, repaired sill splices, skylight frame, and rafters remain unchanged above. By t=7.5s, the worker removes empty cartons, spacers, and tapping block through the camera-side lower edge. Persistent traces: tight plank seams, tiny perimeter expansion gaps, screw heads locked below the finished field, and faint cork dust at edges. Sound layers include rubber tapping knocks, screw cup rattles, spacer clicks, and cardboard carton slides; ambient room tone stays quiet with wind lightly filtered through repaired glass.\n\n视频 7:\nUse the provided first frame and last frame as exact composition anchors. Use IMAGE 7 as the actual first-frame image and IMAGE 8 as the actual last-frame image; every visible action must interpolate between those two frame images without inventing a third layout. The locked interior view preserves the window band, roof hatch skylight, rafters, repaired sills, and finished floor without camera movement. This is continuous construction time-lapse, not real-time footage, shifting from partial battery-lantern warmth to stable installed practical lighting. At t=0s, one lone worker in a solid bright-neon-yellow safety vest, white hardhat, solid dark blue work pants, gloves, and respirator enters from the camera-side lower edge carrying an insulated drill-driver as the single featured fastening tool. The worker cycles around the full visible ceiling perimeter, pressing low-voltage rechargeable LED strip channels tight under the rafters, driving small screws, and snapping frosted diffuser covers into the channels. Progress is measured by ceiling-edge channels extending continuously around all visible upper walls and by warm light blooming evenly from puck lights and lanterns near the end. The finished floor stays protected by folded canvas pads that are removed before the last frame. By t=7.5s, the worker takes the drill-driver, pads, and empty hardware tray out through the camera-side lower edge. Persistent traces: tiny screw heads in strip channels, clipped cable saddles, lantern clamp marks, and shallow pad dust outlines that mostly remain near the floor edge. Audio contains drill-driver whirs, plastic diffuser snaps, cable clip ticks, and ladder-foot scuffs on canvas; ambient room tone is warmed by a faint electrical hum and muffled forest wind.\n\n视频 8:\nUse the provided first frame and last frame as exact composition anchors. Use IMAGE 8 as the actual first-frame image and IMAGE 9 as the actual last-frame image; every visible action must interpolate between those two frame images without inventing a third layout. The interior tripod composition remains fixed, keeping the wraparound window band unblocked, the roof hatch skylight centered overhead, rafters aligned, and finished floor continuous. This is continuous construction time-lapse, not real-time footage, transitioning from full warm practical lighting into dimmed stargazing mode as the window and skylight openings deepen to night tones. At t=0s, one lone worker in a solid bright-neon-yellow safety vest, white hardhat, solid dark blue work pants, gloves, and respirator enters from the camera-side lower edge carrying a hand screwdriver as the single featured tool. The worker then carries in a matte black telescope on a brushed aluminum tripod, extending and locking the tripod legs so the tube aims up toward the repaired roof hatch skylight. Progress is visible as empty floor becomes a complete stargazing layout while warm light gently dims and night deepens beyond the glass. By t=7.5s, the worker removes the screwdriver and empty packing blankets through the camera-side lower edge. Persistent traces: rug compression lines, chair foot marks, crate rub marks, and tripod foot pads on the matte floor. Sounds include screwdriver clicks, folding-leg snaps, wool rug rasping, and tripod locks ticking; ambient quiet shifts to soft night air and faint treetop movement outside.\n\n视频 9:\nUse the provided first frame and last frame as exact composition anchors. Use IMAGE 9 as the actual first-frame image and IMAGE 10 as the actual last-frame image; every visible action must interpolate between those two frame images without inventing a third layout. From inside the completed lookout cabin, the camera remains a stabilized interior tripod view with level pitch and centered vanishing axis, holding the window band, roof hatch skylight, rafters, finished floor, telescope, table, chairs, rug, crates, and mattress platform in their settled positions. No construction agents enter; this reward clip shows only the final lighting and atmosphere settling. The warm installed ceiling strips, puck lights, and wall lanterns rise from dimmed stargazing mode to final practical stabilization, spreading amber light across the cork-backed plank floor, telescope tube, chair frames, star-chart papers, and repaired cedar sill splices. The highly low-sheen finished floor displays heavily blurred, low-gloss, diffused reflections of the warm strip channels, chair legs, and telescope tripod, muted and out of focus with soft falloff near the margins. The visible reward action is continuous: practical fixtures warming, glass edges catching small highlights, star charts softly brightening, and the telescope silhouette becoming crisp beneath the skylight. All objects remain physically still and already placed, with no cuts, fades, or layout changes. Persistent traces: tripod foot pads, rug compression, clean glazing putty ridges, and tiny screw heads in light channels. Near-field sound is delicate: faint switch clicks, quiet electrical hum, subtle plank creaks from cooling wood, and a soft whisper through the skylight hardware; ambient noise is calm night forest air with distant treetop rustle.",
